{
  "teamId": 135,
  "year": 2024,
  "narrative": "The 2024 San Diego Padres were a team that refused to be defined by their stumbles, clawing their way from a disappointing 12-15 April into one of baseball's most dominant second halves. The early weeks were a mixed bag — Fernando Tatis Jr. delivered a stunning go-ahead blast in early April to signal his return to form, but the team sputtered around him, finishing the month well below .500 and searching for an identity.\n\nThe turning point came quietly but unmistakably in May and June. Jurickson Profar emerged as a revelation, launching clutch home run after clutch home run and punctuating a June 24th rally with a bases-clearing single that captured everything the Padres were becoming: opportunistic, resilient, and dangerous in the late innings. Veterans like Manny Machado delivered when it counted, and a young Jackson Merrill announced himself as a genuine star, his bat heating up alongside Profar's through the summer months.\n\nAugust was the Padres at their absolute best — an 18-10 record powered by a power surge from Profar, Merrill, and a deep roster that outscored nearly everyone in baseball. The bullpen showed cracks, particularly Robert Suarez, who surrendered crucial home runs in September, but the rotation anchored by Dylan Cease and a stingy 3.86 ERA kept San Diego in games long enough for their offense to do damage. They closed the regular season 93-69, the best record of the Petco Park era.\n\nOctober brought a Wild Card sweep and a date with the Los Angeles Dodgers in the NLDS — and there, the dream stalled. Shohei Ohtani homered off Cease in Game 1, and a Teoscar Hernández grand slam off Michael King in Game 3 proved the killing blow. David Peralta gave the Padres hope with a two-run shot in Game 2 and a clutch double in Game 3, but the Dodgers were simply too deep. San Diego's 2024 season ended not in failure but in the painful, particular heartbreak of a team that had everything except enough October luck.",
